\section{One Edge May Carry Several Independent Contracts}

A graph edge can transport values while also participating in a clock domain, fixed token rate, tensor shape, state dependence, effect order, and provenance chain. These dimensions should not be compressed into a single vague ``dataflow type.'' Some compose algebraically; others are obligations or metadata. A representative typed edge might print as:

\begin{verbatim}
%samples : stream<tensor<128xf32>>
  {clock = @audio48k, rate = 128/period,
   state_epoch = %frame, effect = read_only,
   origin = ["mix.lucid":12:5, "actor:fft.in"]}
\end{verbatim}

The clock says when logical periods occur, rate says how many tokens participate, shape says each token's value structure, and effect says what evaluation may observe or change. Provenance explains origin but should not change execution. State epoch can prevent a value computed from one snapshot from being reused in another.

\section{Use Separate Lattices and Explicit Conversion Nodes}

The IR design assigns these contracts separate fields and solvers instead of packing them into an opaque type string. Clock inference unifies domains or inserts sample/hold, resampling, or synchronization operations. Rate inference solves integer or rational balance constraints. Shape inference solves extents and broadcasts. Effects form an ordering and interference discipline. State is represented by explicit reads, writes, delays, or region-carried values. Provenance is a many-to-many graph of source occurrences and transformations, not a single line number overwritten at each pass.

